WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.522 --> 00:00:08.025
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.027 --> 00:00:10.449
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.449 --> 00:00:12.946
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.948 --> 00:00:15.374
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:15.375 --> 00:00:17.968
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:20.351
things about the world at the same

00:00:20.353 --> 00:00:22.047
time as improving your English.

00:00:22.438 --> 00:00:25.011
I'm Alastair Budge, and today

00:00:25.013 --> 00:00:27.672
we are talking about the news.

00:00:27.958 --> 00:00:29.642
But not any news.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:33.253
Today, in our last episode of the year, we

00:00:33.255 --> 00:00:36.366
are going to look back at some of the most

00:00:36.426 --> 00:00:41.074
unusual news stories from the UK in 2026.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:44.228
There are churches and murdered dogs,

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:47.025
stolen beer, governments breaking the

00:00:47.026 --> 00:00:50.012
rules, Germans chasing cheese, people

00:00:50.014 --> 00:00:52.997
driving who shouldn't be, and unusual

00:00:53.015 --> 00:00:55.584
things to do while running a marathon.

00:00:56.068 --> 00:00:59.075
We have a lot to cover, so let's not

00:00:59.077 --> 00:01:02.166
waste a minute and get right into it.

00:01:03.757 --> 00:01:06.848
If you have followed the British news

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:09.604
in 2026, you will know that a lot

00:01:09.667 --> 00:01:12.950
of it hasn't exactly been feelgood.

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:16.671
Rows about money, rows about housing,

00:01:16.673 --> 00:01:19.896
rows about who should be running what.

00:01:20.447 --> 00:01:21.392
The usual stuff.

00:01:22.596 --> 00:01:25.646
None of that is going to feature today.

00:01:26.205 --> 00:01:29.569
Today is about the other kind of news.

00:01:29.902 --> 00:01:32.651
The stories that end up on page 23,

00:01:32.653 --> 00:01:35.636
or at the bottom of a website, because

00:01:35.935 --> 00:01:37.641
they aren't particularly important.

00:01:37.971 --> 00:01:40.425
But they are odd, and in some

00:01:40.427 --> 00:01:42.964
cases they are very funny, and

00:01:43.075 --> 00:01:44.981
what better way to end the year

00:01:44.983 --> 00:01:47.133
than by talking about some of them.

00:01:47.693 --> 00:01:51.172
So, our first story takes us to

00:01:51.174 --> 00:01:54.764
Wales, and to a small village in

00:01:54.764 --> 00:01:57.205
the mountains of Snowdonia, or

00:01:57.207 --> 00:01:59.891
Eryri, to give it its Welsh name.

00:02:01.014 --> 00:02:03.714
The village is called Beddgelert.

00:02:04.154 --> 00:02:06.952
It has a few hundred residents, two

00:02:06.954 --> 00:02:10.150
rivers, an old stone bridge, and a small

00:02:10.516 --> 00:02:13.855
800-year-old church called St Mary's.

00:02:14.458 --> 00:02:17.232
And in July of this year, this

00:02:17.234 --> 00:02:19.913
church, St Mary's church, was

00:02:19.914 --> 00:02:22.817
declared the best place in the world.

00:02:23.596 --> 00:02:26.140
Now, you might be thinking, "hmm, I've

00:02:26.142 --> 00:02:28.751
never heard of that before", or perhaps

00:02:28.751 --> 00:02:31.290
you might even look it up and see it only

00:02:31.292 --> 00:02:33.767
has twenty-eight reviews on Google Maps.

00:02:34.603 --> 00:02:36.584
And quite rightly, you might ask

00:02:36.586 --> 00:02:39.122
yourself "I wonder how that was decided".

00:02:40.337 --> 00:02:43.268
I should explain who did the declaring.

00:02:43.799 --> 00:02:45.554
This was decided by the

00:02:45.556 --> 00:02:47.690
users of the website Reddit.

00:02:48.351 --> 00:02:50.255
In case you don't know, it's a

00:02:50.257 --> 00:02:52.285
collection of discussion forums,

00:02:52.399 --> 00:02:54.826
called communities, on every subject

00:02:54.828 --> 00:02:57.321
you can imagine, and probably quite a

00:02:57.321 --> 00:02:59.278
few more you've never even heard of.

00:03:00.231 --> 00:03:02.291
One of these communities

00:03:02.293 --> 00:03:04.522
is called "terrible maps".

00:03:05.346 --> 00:03:08.568
It's devoted, as the name suggests, to

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:11.789
badly drawn, pointless or deliberately

00:03:11.822 --> 00:03:14.473
silly maps, and it is super popular.

00:03:15.677 --> 00:03:19.576
In June, somebody there started a game.

00:03:20.585 --> 00:03:22.833
They took a map of the whole

00:03:22.835 --> 00:03:25.161
world and drew a line through

00:03:25.161 --> 00:03:26.801
it, cutting it in half.

00:03:27.692 --> 00:03:31.197
Members voted on which half to delete.

00:03:31.776 --> 00:03:34.164
Then the surviving half was cut in

00:03:34.166 --> 00:03:36.693
two again, and everyone voted again.

00:03:37.423 --> 00:03:40.746
Round after round, the world got smaller.

00:03:41.075 --> 00:03:42.388
Whole continents went.

00:03:42.644 --> 00:03:45.804
Then countries, then regions, then towns.

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:50.328
It took 29 rounds, and thousands of

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:54.028
votes, and at the end of it the last

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:56.830
place standing on Earth was this

00:03:56.832 --> 00:03:59.851
church in a village in north Wales.

00:04:01.011 --> 00:04:02.219
Why that church?

00:04:03.146 --> 00:04:04.299
It's not really clear.

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:07.684
Reddit seems to have a habit of picking

00:04:07.686 --> 00:04:10.693
an underdog and rallying behind it, partly

00:04:10.693 --> 00:04:12.740
because it's funny, and partly because

00:04:12.742 --> 00:04:14.841
it would be awfully predictable for the

00:04:14.842 --> 00:04:17.301
winner to be the Eiffel Tower or the

00:04:17.303 --> 00:04:20.170
Great Pyramids, or somewhere more obvious.

00:04:21.396 --> 00:04:23.788
The vote reached Wales, word got

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:26.554
around, and even the residents of the

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:29.053
village, many of whom had never even

00:04:29.055 --> 00:04:31.824
heard of Reddit before, joined the party

00:04:32.038 --> 00:04:34.237
and helped the church over the line.

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:37.945
When it was all over, the person who

00:04:37.947 --> 00:04:40.550
had run the game posted the result, and

00:04:40.562 --> 00:04:42.851
asked, "Does this mean the Church of Saint

00:04:42.853 --> 00:04:44.867
Mary is the best place in the world?"

00:04:45.883 --> 00:04:48.603
Their answer: "That's up to you to decide,

00:04:48.605 --> 00:04:50.870
but it does seem my very scientific

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:52.895
method has found a nice candidate."

00:04:54.027 --> 00:04:56.213
The vicar, the Reverend Kim

00:04:56.215 --> 00:04:58.561
Williams, said the result was

00:04:58.562 --> 00:05:00.875
a surprise, but a welcome one.

00:05:01.456 --> 00:05:03.695
The church needs urgent repairs,

00:05:03.697 --> 00:05:06.214
and there is now a fundraising page,

00:05:06.412 --> 00:05:09.224
so if a few thousand Reddit users

00:05:09.226 --> 00:05:12.291
feel like backing up their vote with

00:05:12.292 --> 00:05:14.494
a donation, she will not say no.

00:05:15.793 --> 00:05:17.458
And there's a nice story

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:19.193
about the village itself.

00:05:20.001 --> 00:05:23.081
The name Beddgelert means "Gelert's

00:05:23.083 --> 00:05:26.160
grave", and a five-minute walk from

00:05:26.161 --> 00:05:28.679
the church is the grave in question.

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:32.704
The story goes that Gelert was a

00:05:32.706 --> 00:05:36.109
dog, the faithful hound of a medieval

00:05:36.118 --> 00:05:38.337
Welsh prince called Llywelyn, which

00:05:38.339 --> 00:05:40.303
is an awfully difficult name to

00:05:40.304 --> 00:05:42.163
spell for an English learner, but

00:05:42.165 --> 00:05:44.022
a relatively common one in Wales.

00:05:44.848 --> 00:05:47.445
Anyway, one day, the prince

00:05:47.447 --> 00:05:50.235
went out hunting and left his

00:05:50.245 --> 00:05:52.385
dog guarding his baby son.

00:05:53.151 --> 00:05:55.564
Perhaps not the best idea, you might

00:05:55.566 --> 00:05:57.843
think, but this was 800 years ago.

00:05:59.167 --> 00:06:01.974
When he came back, the cradle was

00:06:01.976 --> 00:06:05.547
overturned, there was no sign of the baby,

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:08.947
and the dog's mouth was covered in blood.

00:06:09.945 --> 00:06:12.464
The prince drew his sword and

00:06:12.466 --> 00:06:14.809
killed the dog on the spot.

00:06:15.910 --> 00:06:17.849
Then he heard a cry.

00:06:18.719 --> 00:06:21.359
The baby was lying unharmed

00:06:21.361 --> 00:06:24.195
under the cradle, and next to

00:06:24.197 --> 00:06:27.175
it was the body of a dead wolf.

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:29.930
The dog had been protecting

00:06:29.932 --> 00:06:31.486
the child all along.

00:06:32.843 --> 00:06:34.864
Now, what's the moral of the story?

00:06:35.503 --> 00:06:36.823
Don't jump to conclusions?

00:06:37.159 --> 00:06:38.648
Don't kill your faithful dog until

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:40.181
you're absolutely sure it's guilty?

00:06:40.771 --> 00:06:42.070
Or just don't leave an infant

00:06:42.072 --> 00:06:43.235
on its own with an animal?

00:06:43.999 --> 00:06:46.342
It's a little unclear, and as you

00:06:46.344 --> 00:06:48.756
might imagine, the story is almost

00:06:48.762 --> 00:06:51.720
certainly made up, most likely by an

00:06:51.722 --> 00:06:54.595
18th-century pub landlord who built

00:06:54.617 --> 00:06:57.155
the "grave" to bring in tourists.

00:06:58.018 --> 00:06:58.599
It worked.

00:06:58.937 --> 00:07:01.493
People are still visiting more than

00:07:01.495 --> 00:07:04.050
200 years later, and now, thanks to

00:07:04.058 --> 00:07:06.203
Reddit, they have two things to see.

00:07:07.098 --> 00:07:09.838
Now, moving from one public vote

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:12.235
to another, story number two

00:07:12.598 --> 00:07:15.276
comes with slightly more at stake.

00:07:16.286 --> 00:07:19.145
If you've been to the UK, you might

00:07:19.147 --> 00:07:22.086
remember that English banknotes each

00:07:22.096 --> 00:07:25.102
have a famous person on the back: Winston

00:07:25.104 --> 00:07:27.889
Churchill on the five pound note, Jane

00:07:27.913 --> 00:07:31.144
Austen on the ten, the painter J.M.W.

00:07:31.185 --> 00:07:33.637
Turner on the twenty, and the

00:07:33.639 --> 00:07:36.766
codebreaker Alan Turing on the fifty.

00:07:37.794 --> 00:07:40.261
In March of this year, the Bank

00:07:40.263 --> 00:07:42.569
of England announced that the

00:07:42.571 --> 00:07:45.403
next series of notes would do away

00:07:45.405 --> 00:07:47.902
with famous people altogether.

00:07:48.611 --> 00:07:51.448
Instead, after a public consultation on

00:07:51.450 --> 00:07:54.504
what the theme should be, it chose nature.

00:07:55.248 --> 00:07:57.548
British wildlife would go on the money.

00:07:58.568 --> 00:08:00.192
Which raised the obvious

00:08:00.194 --> 00:08:01.884
question: which wildlife?

00:08:02.313 --> 00:08:05.673
So in June, the Bank opened another vote,

00:08:05.675 --> 00:08:09.032
and this time it was the public's choice.

00:08:09.831 --> 00:08:12.564
A panel of six wildlife experts

00:08:12.566 --> 00:08:15.561
drew up a shortlist of 18 animals,

00:08:15.838 --> 00:08:18.161
in three categories: mammals,

00:08:18.163 --> 00:08:20.804
birds, and a third group covering

00:08:20.842 --> 00:08:23.186
insects, fish and amphibians.

00:08:24.129 --> 00:08:26.303
The vote ran for a month.

00:08:26.904 --> 00:08:29.275
Nearly half a million people

00:08:29.277 --> 00:08:32.407
responded, and between them they made

00:08:32.492 --> 00:08:34.853
two and a half million selections.

00:08:35.696 --> 00:08:37.009
The results came out

00:08:37.011 --> 00:08:38.454
on the 8th of October.

00:08:39.045 --> 00:08:41.795
The four winners were the Atlantic

00:08:41.797 --> 00:08:44.788
puffin, the barn owl, the buff-tailed

00:08:44.795 --> 00:08:47.125
bumblebee, and the hedgehog.

00:08:48.251 --> 00:08:50.738
Among the losers were the red fox, the

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:53.291
kingfisher, the white-tailed eagle, the

00:08:53.291 --> 00:08:56.250
basking shark, and the Atlantic salmon.

00:08:57.241 --> 00:08:58.878
Each animal will be the

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:00.657
main picture on one note.

00:09:01.422 --> 00:09:03.971
Which animal is worth five pounds and

00:09:03.973 --> 00:09:06.520
which is worth fifty, the Bank hasn't

00:09:06.524 --> 00:09:08.890
said yet, and the first of the new

00:09:08.892 --> 00:09:11.464
notes isn't expected for a few years.

00:09:12.057 --> 00:09:13.973
So, Churchill is out

00:09:13.975 --> 00:09:16.080
and a bumblebee is in.

00:09:16.871 --> 00:09:19.002
Churchill, I suspect, would have had

00:09:19.004 --> 00:09:21.015
something witty to say about that.

00:09:22.005 --> 00:09:25.507
Now, moving on to story number three,

00:09:25.509 --> 00:09:28.726
and this concerns a man and a test

00:09:28.962 --> 00:09:31.347
he never quite got round to taking.

00:09:32.537 --> 00:09:36.476
In April, police in Warwickshire stopped

00:09:36.478 --> 00:09:39.825
a BMW on a main road near the town

00:09:39.861 --> 00:09:42.341
of Bedworth, just outside Coventry.

00:09:43.323 --> 00:09:46.129
The driver was 64 years old.

00:09:47.113 --> 00:09:49.153
The officers asked to see his

00:09:49.155 --> 00:09:51.614
licence, then ran a check, and this

00:09:51.637 --> 00:09:53.369
is where things got interesting.

00:09:54.513 --> 00:09:56.630
He had a licence, of a sort.

00:09:57.210 --> 00:09:58.968
He had applied for what in

00:09:58.970 --> 00:10:00.996
the UK is called a provisional

00:10:00.998 --> 00:10:05.144
licence, in September 1978.

00:10:06.527 --> 00:10:08.998
A provisional licence, in case you

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:11.542
don't know, is a learner's licence.

00:10:11.983 --> 00:10:14.456
It allows you to drive, but only with

00:10:14.458 --> 00:10:16.929
an experienced driver sitting next to

00:10:16.929 --> 00:10:19.999
you and big red "L" plates on the car.

00:10:20.734 --> 00:10:22.860
To drive on your own, like in most

00:10:22.862 --> 00:10:25.048
countries, you have to pass a test.

00:10:25.945 --> 00:10:28.306
He had never passed the test.

00:10:28.669 --> 00:10:29.738
Never even taken it.

00:10:30.113 --> 00:10:32.050
Never, as far as anyone could

00:10:32.052 --> 00:10:34.053
tell, got round to booking it.

00:10:35.304 --> 00:10:37.783
He got his provisional licence as

00:10:37.785 --> 00:10:40.111
a 17-year-old, and then for the

00:10:40.112 --> 00:10:43.245
next 48 years he simply drove.

00:10:43.713 --> 00:10:46.611
No instructor, no L plates, no test,

00:10:46.613 --> 00:10:49.669
apparently without ever being stopped.

00:10:50.968 --> 00:10:53.371
The police reported him for driving

00:10:53.373 --> 00:10:55.636
without a proper licence, and his

00:10:55.639 --> 00:10:58.296
BMW was seized and taken away.

00:10:59.306 --> 00:11:01.066
Whether he has now booked a

00:11:01.068 --> 00:11:02.891
test, the police didn't say.

00:11:03.569 --> 00:11:05.678
One hopes that with almost fifty

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:07.985
years of practice, he should be OK.

00:11:09.211 --> 00:11:12.294
Now, story number four stays on the

00:11:12.296 --> 00:11:15.377
roads, and moves to Birmingham, the

00:11:15.378 --> 00:11:17.978
second biggest city in the UK, and the

00:11:17.980 --> 00:11:20.577
setting for the Peaky Blinders series.

00:11:21.817 --> 00:11:24.782
In 2021, Birmingham introduced

00:11:24.784 --> 00:11:28.143
something called a Clean Air Zone.

00:11:29.006 --> 00:11:30.608
The idea is simple.

00:11:30.948 --> 00:11:33.872
If you drive an older, more polluting

00:11:33.874 --> 00:11:36.795
vehicle into the city centre, you pay

00:11:36.832 --> 00:11:40.153
a daily charge: eight pounds for a car,

00:11:40.155 --> 00:11:43.219
fifty pounds for a lorry or a coach.

00:11:44.042 --> 00:11:46.649
Lots of cities have similar schemes,

00:11:46.651 --> 00:11:48.966
and they tend to be an effective

00:11:48.966 --> 00:11:51.074
way of improving the air quality.

00:11:52.324 --> 00:11:55.380
In Birmingham, cameras read your number

00:11:55.382 --> 00:11:58.358
plate as you drive in, and you need to

00:11:58.358 --> 00:12:00.479
remember to go online and pay the fee.

00:12:01.083 --> 00:12:03.723
If you don't pay within a few days,

00:12:03.725 --> 00:12:06.363
you get a fine of 120 pounds, which

00:12:06.363 --> 00:12:08.898
is reduced to 60 if you pay quickly.

00:12:09.780 --> 00:12:13.234
Anyway, in June of this year, the

00:12:13.236 --> 00:12:16.479
BBC published the results of an

00:12:16.527 --> 00:12:18.636
investigation into who had been

00:12:18.638 --> 00:12:20.677
paying the most in Birmingham.

00:12:22.094 --> 00:12:24.789
And the answer was Birmingham

00:12:24.791 --> 00:12:26.927
City Council, the local

00:12:26.931 --> 00:12:27.969
government, essentially.

00:12:28.789 --> 00:12:31.684
The council's own vehicles had driven

00:12:31.686 --> 00:12:34.187
into its own Clean Air Zone, and

00:12:34.188 --> 00:12:38.190
been photographed by its own cameras,

00:12:38.192 --> 00:12:41.867
3,262 times since the zone opened.

00:12:43.225 --> 00:12:44.435
And remember, these

00:12:44.437 --> 00:12:46.026
aren't just any vehicles.

00:12:46.291 --> 00:12:49.015
They are older, more polluting vehicles:

00:12:49.017 --> 00:12:51.466
cars that are more than twenty years

00:12:51.467 --> 00:12:53.534
old if they are petrol, and more than

00:12:53.536 --> 00:12:55.546
eleven years old if they are diesel.

00:12:56.754 --> 00:13:01.954
In charges and fines, the council

00:13:01.956 --> 00:13:06.523
had paid 472,253 pounds, more

00:13:06.524 --> 00:13:08.786
than half a million euros.

00:13:09.481 --> 00:13:11.909
Somewhat hypocritical, you might think.

00:13:12.424 --> 00:13:14.566
And certainly wasteful, as it was

00:13:14.568 --> 00:13:16.838
using public money to pay fines for

00:13:16.843 --> 00:13:19.293
a policy it had brought in itself.

00:13:20.565 --> 00:13:23.090
But, if it was being fined, you

00:13:23.092 --> 00:13:26.104
might be thinking, who was it paying?

00:13:27.410 --> 00:13:29.821
The answer is…itself,

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:31.526
in a roundabout way.

00:13:32.901 --> 00:13:35.499
Birmingham City Council administers

00:13:35.501 --> 00:13:37.725
the scheme, so it collects all

00:13:37.726 --> 00:13:39.556
the income from it, with a slice

00:13:39.558 --> 00:13:41.386
going to the central government.

00:13:42.255 --> 00:13:44.373
There are some restrictions on what

00:13:44.375 --> 00:13:46.611
it can be spent on, and it can't just

00:13:46.612 --> 00:13:49.316
go back into the general expenses

00:13:49.318 --> 00:13:52.430
pot, but the council was, essentially,

00:13:52.766 --> 00:13:54.829
breaking its own rules, paying

00:13:54.831 --> 00:13:57.099
the fines, and keeping the money.

00:13:58.370 --> 00:14:00.764
Our fifth story is about a crime,

00:14:00.766 --> 00:14:03.304
and it involves beer, which is just

00:14:03.304 --> 00:14:05.622
a great combination for a news story.

00:14:06.675 --> 00:14:08.975
Guinness, as you will almost

00:14:08.977 --> 00:14:11.767
certainly know, is the black Irish

00:14:11.805 --> 00:14:14.277
stout with the creamy white top.

00:14:14.725 --> 00:14:17.145
It is enormously popular in Britain.

00:14:18.134 --> 00:14:20.411
And beer, Guinness included,

00:14:20.413 --> 00:14:22.281
is now quite expensive.

00:14:23.215 --> 00:14:27.251
In 2026, the average price of a pint in a

00:14:27.253 --> 00:14:31.287
British pub was five pounds and 41 pence.

00:14:32.675 --> 00:14:35.732
Before it gets to the pub, it sits

00:14:35.734 --> 00:14:38.700
in a warehouse, in metal kegs, on

00:14:38.726 --> 00:14:40.826
trailers, waiting to be delivered.

00:14:42.263 --> 00:14:46.417
And on the night of Monday the 31st of

00:14:46.419 --> 00:14:50.570
August, two men drove two lorries into

00:14:50.570 --> 00:14:53.422
an industrial park in Runcorn, a town in

00:14:53.424 --> 00:14:56.131
Cheshire in the north west of England.

00:14:56.982 --> 00:14:59.772
They hitched up two trailers,

00:14:59.774 --> 00:15:01.696
and drove out again.

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:05.855
In the trailers were around

00:15:05.857 --> 00:15:08.076
800 kegs of Guinness.

00:15:08.625 --> 00:15:12.720
That is roughly 70,000 pints, worth

00:15:12.722 --> 00:15:16.933
almost 400,000 pounds at pub prices.

00:15:18.369 --> 00:15:20.839
The trailers turned up a few days later,

00:15:20.841 --> 00:15:22.938
about twenty-five kilometres away.

00:15:23.922 --> 00:15:26.363
One had been dumped in a residential

00:15:26.365 --> 00:15:29.210
street shortly after three in the morning.

00:15:29.755 --> 00:15:31.541
The other was left on an

00:15:31.543 --> 00:15:33.922
industrial road at midday on the

00:15:33.922 --> 00:15:35.886
Wednesday, in broad daylight.

00:15:36.759 --> 00:15:38.569
Both were empty.

00:15:39.865 --> 00:15:41.669
Police put out descriptions

00:15:41.671 --> 00:15:42.938
of the two drivers.

00:15:43.568 --> 00:15:45.697
One was white, with a short

00:15:45.699 --> 00:15:47.905
dark beard and a woolly hat.

00:15:48.410 --> 00:15:50.392
The other wore a cap.

00:15:51.405 --> 00:15:54.742
Officials also made a rather unusual

00:15:54.744 --> 00:15:57.431
public appeal: anyone who had

00:15:57.431 --> 00:15:59.249
recently been offered a very large

00:15:59.251 --> 00:16:01.121
quantity of Guinness at a very good

00:16:01.122 --> 00:16:03.613
price was urged to get in touch.

00:16:04.936 --> 00:16:07.318
The officer leading the investigation,

00:16:07.320 --> 00:16:09.199
Detective Sergeant McClatchey,

00:16:09.577 --> 00:16:11.578
said that his team would settle

00:16:11.580 --> 00:16:13.515
for nothing less than the full

00:16:13.534 --> 00:16:15.217
recovery of everything stolen.

00:16:16.369 --> 00:16:18.558
He also pointed out that Guinness

00:16:18.560 --> 00:16:20.681
may well be good for you, as the

00:16:20.681 --> 00:16:23.033
old advertising slogan says, but

00:16:23.035 --> 00:16:25.165
only once it's been paid for.

00:16:26.355 --> 00:16:29.108
Now, as of the time of recording,

00:16:29.110 --> 00:16:31.444
the beer has not been found.

00:16:31.901 --> 00:16:33.595
And perhaps the evidence

00:16:33.597 --> 00:16:35.500
has already been destroyed.

00:16:36.518 --> 00:16:39.188
A keg of Guinness doesn't keep for ever.

00:16:39.686 --> 00:16:41.963
So somewhere in the north west of

00:16:41.965 --> 00:16:44.517
England, there was either a very busy

00:16:44.539 --> 00:16:48.044
illegal pub, a very large amount of flat

00:16:48.046 --> 00:16:51.461
stout, or two incredibly drunk thieves.

00:16:51.931 --> 00:16:53.149
Perhaps all three.

00:16:54.573 --> 00:16:56.563
Story number six takes us to

00:16:56.565 --> 00:16:58.767
Gloucestershire, in the west of

00:16:58.767 --> 00:17:01.568
England, and to one of the oldest and

00:17:01.570 --> 00:17:04.445
oddest sporting events in the country.

00:17:05.567 --> 00:17:07.527
Just outside Gloucester there

00:17:07.529 --> 00:17:09.622
is a hill called Cooper's Hill.

00:17:10.276 --> 00:17:12.577
It is very steep, so steep that in

00:17:12.579 --> 00:17:15.080
places you can barely stand up on it.

00:17:15.815 --> 00:17:19.051
And every year, on the last Monday

00:17:19.053 --> 00:17:22.191
in May, someone stands at the top

00:17:22.422 --> 00:17:24.908
with a large round cheese, a Double

00:17:24.910 --> 00:17:27.395
Gloucester weighing about three and

00:17:27.396 --> 00:17:30.906
a half kilograms, and rolls it down.

00:17:31.725 --> 00:17:34.570
Then a group of people run after it.

00:17:35.225 --> 00:17:37.148
You might have seen clips of this.

00:17:37.671 --> 00:17:40.218
The cheese, it is said, reaches

00:17:40.220 --> 00:17:42.930
speeds of 100 kilometres an hour.

00:17:43.363 --> 00:17:45.120
Nobody has ever caught it.

00:17:45.645 --> 00:17:47.840
The winner is simply the first

00:17:47.842 --> 00:17:50.109
person to reach the bottom, and

00:17:50.363 --> 00:17:51.988
they get to keep the cheese.

00:17:53.113 --> 00:17:55.116
If you've seen footage of this,

00:17:55.118 --> 00:17:57.183
you'll know how dangerous it is.

00:17:57.659 --> 00:18:00.073
People are essentially rolling and

00:18:00.075 --> 00:18:02.842
falling down this extremely steep hill.

00:18:03.293 --> 00:18:05.281
There are broken bones every

00:18:05.283 --> 00:18:07.410
year, and there are paramedics

00:18:07.450 --> 00:18:08.896
waiting at the bottom of the hill.

00:18:09.862 --> 00:18:13.240
To date, miraculously, nobody has died.

00:18:13.897 --> 00:18:15.966
It is so dangerous, in fact,

00:18:15.968 --> 00:18:18.257
that in 2010 the official event

00:18:18.277 --> 00:18:20.884
was cancelled on safety grounds.

00:18:21.883 --> 00:18:23.993
It carries on anyway, with no

00:18:23.995 --> 00:18:26.393
official organiser, and thousands

00:18:26.417 --> 00:18:28.258
of people come to watch.

00:18:28.936 --> 00:18:32.489
Now, for most of this century, cheese

00:18:32.491 --> 00:18:35.946
rolling has had one undisputed king.

00:18:36.694 --> 00:18:38.844
His name is Chris Anderson,

00:18:38.846 --> 00:18:41.232
and he is a local man from the

00:18:41.240 --> 00:18:42.742
village at the bottom of the hill.

00:18:43.215 --> 00:18:47.215
And between 2005 and 2022 he won

00:18:47.217 --> 00:18:51.466
23 races, which is a world record.

00:18:52.252 --> 00:18:54.166
Then he retired.

00:18:54.923 --> 00:18:58.040
And in his place came a German.

00:18:59.221 --> 00:19:02.439
Tom Kopke is a 24-year-old YouTuber

00:19:02.441 --> 00:19:05.473
from Munich, perhaps better known

00:19:05.476 --> 00:19:07.795
by his online name of Tooleko.

00:19:08.572 --> 00:19:11.973
He won the men's race in 2024, and again

00:19:11.975 --> 00:19:15.544
in 2025, and then he did something cheeky.

00:19:16.053 --> 00:19:18.876
He publicly challenged Chris Anderson

00:19:18.878 --> 00:19:21.852
to come out of retirement and race him.

00:19:23.270 --> 00:19:26.591
Anderson, who was 38, agreed.

00:19:27.174 --> 00:19:30.408
So on the 25th of May this year,

00:19:30.410 --> 00:19:33.238
the two of them stood at the

00:19:33.289 --> 00:19:35.423
top of Cooper's Hill, the cheese

00:19:35.425 --> 00:19:37.558
was released, and off they went.

00:19:38.320 --> 00:19:39.144
Kopke won.

00:19:39.585 --> 00:19:40.118
Just.

00:19:40.677 --> 00:19:43.176
He and Anderson were neck and neck

00:19:43.178 --> 00:19:46.116
almost to the bottom, but the German got

00:19:46.117 --> 00:19:48.681
there first, for the third year running.

00:19:49.705 --> 00:19:51.292
"This year's cheese," he said

00:19:51.294 --> 00:19:53.262
afterwards, "will taste the sweetest

00:19:53.272 --> 00:19:55.220
of all the cheeses I have won."

00:19:56.455 --> 00:19:58.153
There was some consolation

00:19:58.155 --> 00:19:59.721
for the Anderson family.

00:20:00.287 --> 00:20:02.348
The children's race was won by

00:20:02.350 --> 00:20:04.615
Chris's 11-year-old son, William,

00:20:04.992 --> 00:20:07.171
and the teenagers' race by his

00:20:07.173 --> 00:20:09.132
16-year-old nephew, Xander.

00:20:10.236 --> 00:20:12.438
The women's race, I should add,

00:20:12.440 --> 00:20:14.568
was won by a Frenchwoman, Alix

00:20:14.587 --> 00:20:16.793
Heugas, from the Basque Country.

00:20:17.389 --> 00:20:19.888
So, the men's cheese went to Germany,

00:20:19.890 --> 00:20:21.846
the women's cheese to France,

00:20:22.052 --> 00:20:23.954
and the people of Gloucestershire

00:20:23.956 --> 00:20:25.684
went home with the children's.

00:20:26.882 --> 00:20:29.088
OK then, for our final story,

00:20:29.090 --> 00:20:31.371
we stay in the world of sport,

00:20:31.646 --> 00:20:33.399
or something close to it.

00:20:34.348 --> 00:20:37.133
On the 26th of April, the London Marathon

00:20:37.135 --> 00:20:39.578
took place, as it does every spring.

00:20:40.433 --> 00:20:44.167
59,830 people finished it, which is a

00:20:44.169 --> 00:20:48.203
world record for any marathon, anywhere.

00:20:49.624 --> 00:20:52.391
As you might know, the record was broken

00:20:52.393 --> 00:20:54.813
for the fastest marathon ever, with

00:20:54.815 --> 00:20:59.333
Sabastian Sawe breaking the two-hour mark,

00:20:59.335 --> 00:21:03.744
and coming in at an eye-watering 1:59:30.

00:21:04.493 --> 00:21:05.697
Obviously, that was the

00:21:05.699 --> 00:21:07.058
biggest achievement of the

00:21:07.058 --> 00:21:09.653
day, but there were others.

00:21:10.922 --> 00:21:13.262
The London Marathon is the biggest

00:21:13.264 --> 00:21:15.808
single event for silly world records.

00:21:16.537 --> 00:21:18.667
Guinness World Records sends its

00:21:18.669 --> 00:21:21.262
judges, and runners turn up in costume,

00:21:21.594 --> 00:21:24.158
each one trying to be the fastest

00:21:24.160 --> 00:21:26.645
person ever to run 42 kilometres

00:21:26.903 --> 00:21:28.460
dressed as something ridiculous.

00:21:29.686 --> 00:21:31.098
This year, Guinness

00:21:31.100 --> 00:21:32.956
confirmed dozens of them.

00:21:33.636 --> 00:21:36.026
There is now a world record for the

00:21:36.028 --> 00:21:38.485
fastest marathon dressed as a train.

00:21:38.917 --> 00:21:41.097
It is four hours, 43 minutes

00:21:41.099 --> 00:21:43.432
and 46 seconds, and it belongs

00:21:43.432 --> 00:21:45.484
to a man called Iain Grigor.

00:21:46.460 --> 00:21:48.614
The fastest marathon dressed as a

00:21:48.616 --> 00:21:51.160
microphone: three hours and 35 minutes.

00:21:51.524 --> 00:21:53.471
Dressed as an emoji: three

00:21:53.473 --> 00:21:55.044
hours and 31 minutes.

00:21:55.513 --> 00:21:58.008
Dressed as a sweet, as in a piece of

00:21:58.010 --> 00:22:00.919
confectionery: three hours and 24 minutes.

00:22:01.865 --> 00:22:05.195
A man called Harley Smith ran it in

00:22:05.197 --> 00:22:08.905
two hours and 47 minutes wearing Crocs,

00:22:09.191 --> 00:22:11.294
the rubber shoes with holes in them.

00:22:12.260 --> 00:22:14.021
For context, that is faster

00:22:14.023 --> 00:22:15.717
than most people could run

00:22:15.717 --> 00:22:17.166
it in proper running shoes.

00:22:18.423 --> 00:22:20.957
A man called Mark Goulder ran

00:22:20.959 --> 00:22:23.753
it blindfolded, tied to a guide,

00:22:24.071 --> 00:22:26.600
in three hours and 16 minutes.

00:22:27.612 --> 00:22:30.453
A man called Chris Green, known as

00:22:30.455 --> 00:22:33.378
"Rhino Boy", set the record for the

00:22:33.380 --> 00:22:36.356
most marathons run in the same costume.

00:22:37.261 --> 00:22:40.167
He has now run 132 marathons

00:22:40.169 --> 00:22:42.657
dressed as a rhinoceros.

00:22:43.657 --> 00:22:46.367
And perhaps the strangest belongs

00:22:46.369 --> 00:22:49.078
to a man called Simon Fannon, who

00:22:49.080 --> 00:22:51.538
ran the whole thing while knitting.

00:22:52.442 --> 00:22:55.189
He set off with a pair of needles

00:22:55.191 --> 00:22:58.270
and a ball of wool, ran 42 kilometres

00:22:58.532 --> 00:23:00.762
through the streets of London, and

00:23:00.764 --> 00:23:03.189
crossed the finish line with a scarf.

00:23:04.084 --> 00:23:07.882
It measured 5 metres and 59 centimetres,

00:23:07.884 --> 00:23:11.585
and took him five hours and 48 minutes.

00:23:12.377 --> 00:23:14.220
Slow, for a marathon.

00:23:14.848 --> 00:23:16.674
Very quick, for a scarf.

00:23:18.533 --> 00:23:21.732
OK, then, that is it for today's episode

00:23:21.734 --> 00:23:24.612
on the unusual British news of 2026.

00:23:24.943 --> 00:23:27.236
This episode is set to be released on

00:23:27.238 --> 00:23:29.591
Christmas Day, so if you are listening

00:23:29.592 --> 00:23:31.707
to it on the 25th of December, let

00:23:31.709 --> 00:23:33.885
me wish you a very Merry Christmas.

00:23:34.230 --> 00:23:37.228
And as this is the last episode of 2026,

00:23:37.230 --> 00:23:40.002
let me take this opportunity to thank

00:23:40.002 --> 00:23:42.244
you for listening, thank you for being a

00:23:42.246 --> 00:23:44.429
part of this weird and wonderful world,

00:23:44.752 --> 00:23:47.022
and you will hear from me next year.

00:23:47.961 --> 00:23:50.426
You've been listening to English Learning

00:23:50.428 --> 00:23:52.710
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:23:53.006 --> 00:23:55.989
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:23:55.991 --> 00:23:58.736
I'll catch you in the next episode.
